          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only if a bagger kit exists for that exact mower and deck. Factory baggers are designed for a specific deck width, construction and model year, so a kit for one mower rarely fits another. Universal aftermarket baggers exist, but factory kits include the correct chute and mounting hardware for your deck."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many bushels does a zero turn bagger hold?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most twin baggers for 42- to 46-inch decks hold about 6 to 6.5 bushels, roughly 8 cubic feet. Triple baggers for 48- to 54-inch decks typically hold 9 to 11 bushels. One bushel is about 1.24 cubic feet, so an 11-bushel bagger holds about 13.7 cubic feet."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a powered bagger worth it on a zero turn?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It is worth it if you regularly bag wet grass or heavy leaves on two acres or more. The blower keeps the chute from plugging and packs the bins tighter. For dry clippings and moderate leaves on smaller yards, a passive bagger is cheaper, lighter and does the job well."
          }
