          <p>
            Those numbers matter most in leaf season. Grass clippings from a weekly mow compact well, and a 6.5-bushel bagger on a 42-inch deck typically handles a quarter to half an acre of normal summer growth before it needs emptying, depending on how long the grass was. Leaves are a different story. Whole leaves are bulky and light, and even shredded by the blades they fill bins several times faster than clippings. On a property with a few large oaks or maples, a twin bagger can fill in a couple of passes along the tree line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three bins beat two for more than raw capacity. Each bin is smaller and lighter, so emptying is easier on your back, and the load sits more evenly across the back of the mower. Twin baggers on wider decks tend to fill one bin faster than the other, and you end up emptying a half-full pair to clear a full one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Matching capacity to your yard</h3>

          <p>
            As a rough guide, a 6- to 6.5-bushel twin bagger is fine for clippings on lawns under an acre and light leaf drops. A 9-bushel triple bagger suits one to two acres with a moderate leaf fall. An 11-bushel unit, or a powered bagger, is the right tool for two acres or more of heavy leaves. If you are over that, a tow-behind sweeper or a dedicated leaf vacuum will move leaves faster than any bagger on a mower.
          </p>
