        <div id="powered-vs-passive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Powered vs Passive Baggers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every bagger on our ranked list is passive. It relies entirely on the airflow from the mower's blades to push clippings up the chute and into the bins. That works well when the deck is designed for it, the blades are high-lift bagging blades, and the grass and leaves are dry. It works badly in wet grass, long grass, and thick wet leaves, because the blades cannot generate enough airflow to move heavy material up the hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            A powered bagger adds a blower, usually driven by a belt off the engine, that pulls material out of the deck and blows it into the bins. That suction keeps the chute clear in conditions that would plug a passive system, and it packs the bins tighter, so you get more material per dump. Ariens sells a 2-bucket powered bagger, part number 81504500, for its IKON 42- and 52-inch zero turns; retailers list it at 3.7 bushels of capacity, and its listed price has run from about $1,599 to $1,849, which is more than twice the price of most passive kits.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powered baggers are almost all dealer or home-center products tied to gas mowers. None of them is available on Amazon under a verifiable listing, which is why none appears in our ranked picks. If you bag heavy wet leaves every fall on two acres or more, a powered bagger is worth the money, and that may decide which brand of mower you buy. If you mostly bag dry clippings and moderate leaves, a passive triple bagger is cheaper, lighter and simpler, with no extra belt to wear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery zero turns use passive baggers only. There is no engine to drive a blower, and a separate electric blower would cost range. If you are considering an EGO Z6 or a Greenworks CrossoverZ with a bagger, expect it to behave like any passive system: very good in dry conditions, frustrating in wet.
          </p>

