        <div id="fitment" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Fitment: Part Numbers, Deck Type and Model Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fitment is where most bagger purchases go wrong. Manufacturers build bagger kits around a specific deck, and the variables are deck width, deck construction, and model year. Get any one of them wrong and the chute will not line up with the discharge opening, the frame will not bolt to the rear of the mower, or the bins will hit the seat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deck construction trips up the most buyers. Husqvarna's Z254 has been sold with both a stamped 54-inch deck and a 54-inch ClearCut fabricated deck, and the two take different baggers: 592620801 for the stamped version and 591111401 for the ClearCut fabricated version. Both hold 9 bushels; neither fits the other mower. The only reliable way to know which you have is to read the model number on the mower's ID tag and match it to the manufacturer's parts lookup.
          </p>

          <p>
            Model year matters too. Cub Cadet's 19B70054100 double bagger replaces 19A70054100, which Home Depot lists for Ultima ZT1 zero turns from 2019 onward. Older Cub Cadet RZT mowers used different baggers, even at the same deck width. Cub Cadet's 48-inch triple bagger fits only Ultima ZTX and ZTXS mowers from 2020 on, and steering-wheel ZTXS models need adapter kit 490-950-C062 as well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Do not confuse similar part numbers</h3>

          <p>
            Several brands use near-identical numbers for different products. EGO's ABK4200 is for the 42-inch Z6 zero turn, while the ABK4200T is for its 42-inch T6 lawn tractor. Toro sells separate twin baggers for its TimeCutter 42-inch stamped deck (79410) and the 50- and 54-inch IronForged deck (79412), and the 79412 is listed at 6.43 bushels. John Deere's BUC11342 two-bag system for select 42-inch Z300 mowers is listed at 6.5 bushels, with BUC11344 for 48-inch Z300 decks. None of the Toro or Deere zero turn baggers was available on Amazon under a verifiable listing when we checked, so buy those from a dealer and confirm fit against your serial number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Aftermarket and universal baggers are widely listed online. Some work, but a factory kit is built for your deck's discharge geometry, includes the right chute and mounting hardware, and in some cases the counterweights and blades too. For a several-hundred-dollar purchase that has to bolt to a specific deck, the factory part is worth paying for.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-balance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Rear Weight, Counterweights and Hills
          </h2>

          <p>
            A bagger changes how a zero turn handles. The frame, chute and empty bins add weight behind the rear axle, and full bins add a lot more, since wet grass and packed leaves are heavy. On a zero turn, the rear wheels carry most of the weight anyway. Add a full bagger and the front casters can get light enough to skip on bumps and lose grip on slopes.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why some factory kits include counterweights. EGO's ABK4200 ships with counterweights that keep the mower balanced when the bins are full, and other brands sell front weight kits separately or recommend them in the bagger manual. If your kit calls for counterweights, fit them. They are not optional extras; they are how the manufacturer keeps the machine within its stability limits with a bagger on.
          </p>

          <p>
            Slopes need more care with a bagger than without. Mow up and down slopes rather than across them, slow down as the bins fill, and do the hilly sections first, while the bins are still empty. Never mow a slope with full bins if you would not mow it with a passenger on the back. The manufacturer's slope rating, typically 15 degrees or less for residential zero turns, assumes correct setup, and a full bagger with no front weight is not that. Our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-hills/">mower for hills guide</a> covers machines better suited to steep yards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tire pressure matters too. Check the rear tire pressure against the manual after fitting the bagger, since an underinflated rear tire on one side tilts the deck and the cut. Some owners also find a slightly higher cut setting stops the deck from scalping when the rear is weighed down by full bins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leaf-season" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Leaf Season: When to Bag, Mulch or Sweep
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most people buy a zero turn bagger for leaves, not grass. That is a good use of one, with limits. A bagger works best on a dry layer of leaves a few inches deep, mowed often enough that the leaves never mat down. Shredded by the blades, leaves pack into bins much more densely than whole ones, so a bagger can collect a surprising volume per dump. The trouble starts when leaves are wet, thick, or matted after rain.
          </p>

          <p>
            The better strategy for most yards is a mix. Through early and mid fall, mulch light leaf drops straight back into the lawn on every mow; a mulching kit and sharp blades turn a light layer into fine pieces that break down over winter and feed the soil. When the drop gets heavy, switch to the bagger. Our <a href="/best-mulching-mower/">mulching mower guide</a> explains when mulching stops working and leaves start smothering the grass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a sweeper or blower beats a bagger</h3>

          <p>
            If your property is two acres or more under mature trees, a mower bagger will not keep up in peak season. A tow-behind lawn sweeper holds about twice what the biggest zero turn bagger holds and does not depend on blade airflow, though it struggles with wet leaves too. A backpack blower is still the fastest way to move leaves out of beds and corners where no mower fits; our <a href="/best-leaf-blower/">leaf blower guide</a> covers the strongest options.
          </p>

          <p>
            A practical fall routine looks like this: blow leaves out of beds and onto open lawn, mow with the bagger when everything is dry, and dump directly onto a compost pile or a tarp. Mow every few days in peak drop rather than waiting for a weekend. Thin layers bag cleanly. A foot of wet maple leaves will plug any passive bagger, no matter the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-mower" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Zero Turn to Buy If You Plan to Bag
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you are shopping for a zero turn and know you will bag, let the bagger narrow the field. The strongest combinations are the ones where the factory bagger is large, easy to buy, and designed for a deck that moves air well. On that basis, three setups stand out in this guide: a Cub Cadet Ultima ZTX 48 with the 11-bushel 49A70001100, a Husqvarna Z254F with the 9-bushel ClearCut 591111401, and a Husqvarna Z254 with the 9-bushel stamped 592620801.
          </p>

          <p>
            For lawns under an acre, a 42- or 46-inch Cub Cadet Ultima ZT1 with the 6.5-bushel 19B70054100 is the value setup. The mower is common, parts are everywhere, and the bagger is one of the cheapest factory kits here. Our <a href="/best-residential-zero-turn-mower/">residential zero turn guide</a> covers how the ZT1 compares with other mowers in its price band.
          </p>

          <p>
            For battery zero turns, the choice is narrower. EGO's 42-inch Z6 with the ABK4200 is the most complete electric bagging setup, since it includes blades and counterweights, and Greenworks' 7504802 covers its 42-inch CrossoverZ. Both are passive, 6- to 6.5-bushel systems, and both cost range. They make sense on a lawn of an acre or less where you mostly bag clippings, not on a big leaf property. Our <a href="/best-electric-zero-turn-mower/">electric zero turn guide</a> compares the battery mowers themselves.
          </p>

          <p>
            If heavy wet leaves are your main job, look at dealer-sold mowers with powered baggers, such as Ariens' IKON line, even though they cost more. A powered blower that keeps the chute clear in wet conditions is worth more in a hard fall than an extra two bushels of passive capacity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What a Bagger Really Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Factory zero turn baggers are not cheap. Among the prices we could verify, EGO's ABK4200 has been listed around $516 at one retailer, Husqvarna's ClearCut 591111401 around $740 to $836, and Toro's 79412 50/54-inch twin bagger at $949 on Toro's own page. Powered baggers start around $1,600. Those numbers make a bagger one of the largest accessory purchases you will make for the mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for the extras, too. High-lift bagging blades improve airflow and are often recommended with a bagger, and they wear like any blade; our <a href="/best-lawn-mower-blades/">lawn mower blades guide</a> explains the difference between high-lift, standard and mulching blades. Replacement mesh bags, chute hoses and latches wear over the years, especially if the bagger lives outdoors. Counterweights or front weight kits may be extra on some brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Storage is a real cost. A triple bagger frame and bins take up a garage corner when not on the mower, and fitting and removing it usually takes some minutes with tools, so most owners leave it on for the whole leaf season. Measure the mower's overall length with the bagger fitted before you assume it still fits in the shed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compare the bagger against the alternatives before you buy. A tow-behind lawn sweeper costs less than most factory baggers, holds more, and works behind any mower with a hitch. A mulching kit costs far less again and handles light leaf drops with no dumping at all. A bagger is the right buy when you want clippings and leaves off the lawn on every mow, with no extra towing and no extra machine, and when your mower's factory kit is a size that matches your yard.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering by deck width alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two 54-inch decks from the same brand can take different baggers. Husqvarna's stamped Z254 uses 592620801 and the fabricated ClearCut Z254F uses 591111401. Read the model number on the mower's ID tag and match it on the manufacturer's parts lookup before ordering a several-hundred-dollar kit.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Bagging wet grass and leaves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Passive baggers rely on blade airflow, and wet, heavy material is exactly what that airflow cannot lift. The chute plugs, the deck fills and the cut gets ragged. Bag when the lawn is dry, mow more often in peak leaf drop, and mulch light layers instead of bagging them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the counterweights</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full bagger moves a lot of weight behind the rear axle and lightens the front casters. If your kit ships with counterweights or the manual calls for a front weight kit, fit it. On slopes, a zero turn with full bins and no front weight is outside the setup its slope rating assumes.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping standard blades on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard or mulching blades move less air than high-lift bagging blades, and airflow is what fills the bins. Some kits include bagging blades and some do not. If yours does not, buy the manufacturer's high-lift blades for your deck, and keep them sharp, since dull blades shred less and plug more.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a twin bagger for a leaf-heavy acre</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 6.5-bushel twin bagger fills in a few passes under mature trees. If leaves are the main job on an acre or more, buy a mower whose factory bagger is a 9- or 11-bushel triple, consider a powered bagger, or pair the mower with a tow-behind sweeper instead.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

