        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which zero turn mower has the best bagger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Among factory baggers sold on Amazon, Cub Cadet's 49A70001100 for 48-inch Ultima ZTX mowers has the most capacity at 11 bushels. Husqvarna's 9-bushel triple baggers for the Z254 and Z254F are close behind. For heavy wet leaves, dealer-sold powered baggers such as Ariens' IKON unit handle conditions passive baggers cannot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put a bagger on any zero turn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if a bagger kit exists for that exact mower and deck. Factory baggers are designed for a specific deck width, construction and model year, so a kit for one mower rarely fits another. Universal aftermarket baggers exist, but factory kits include the correct chute and mounting hardware for your deck.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many bushels does a zero turn bagger hold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most twin baggers for 42- to 46-inch decks hold about 6 to 6.5 bushels, roughly 8 cubic feet. Triple baggers for 48- to 54-inch decks typically hold 9 to 11 bushels. One bushel is about 1.24 cubic feet, so an 11-bushel bagger holds about 13.7 cubic feet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a powered bagger worth it on a zero turn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it if you regularly bag wet grass or heavy leaves on two acres or more. The blower keeps the chute from plugging and packs the bins tighter. For dry clippings and moderate leaves on smaller yards, a passive bagger is cheaper, lighter and does the job well.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a zero turn bagger good for leaves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for dry leaves mowed often enough that they never mat. The blades shred them, so they pack tightly. Heavy, wet or matted leaves will plug a passive bagger. Mulch light drops back into the lawn, bag the heavy ones on dry days, and use a blower or sweeper for big properties.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need special blades to bag with a zero turn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                High-lift bagging blades are recommended for most baggers because they move more air, which is what lifts clippings up the chute. Some kits, like EGO's ABK4200, include them. Others, like Cub Cadet's 19B70054100, are listed as working with existing blades, but high-lift blades generally bag better.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an electric zero turn use a bagger?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. EGO sells the ABK4200 for its 42-inch Z6 and the ABK5200 for the 52-inch, and Greenworks sells the 7504802 for its 42-inch CrossoverZ. All are passive baggers. Bagging uses more battery than mulching or side discharge, so expect fewer acres per charge while bagging.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

